==English==

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|la|Martialis||Roman cognomen}}, from {{m|la|martiālis||belonging or dedicated to the Mars, the Roman god of war, or to war}}, from {{m|la|Mārs||the god Mars; the planet Mars}} + {{m|la|-ālis|pos=''suffix forming adjectives of relationship from nouns''}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈmɑːʃəl/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈmɑɹʃəl/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-martial.ogg|a=GA}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɑː(ɹ)ʃəl|s=2}}
* {{homophones|en|marshal|martial}}
* {{hyphenation|en|Mart|ial}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{given name|en|male|from=Latin}}, narrowly applied to certain [[historic]] persons (but some of its foreign cognates are modern given names).
#: {{ux|en|Saint Martial was the first bishop of Limoges, circa 250}}
# An Anglicized [[cognomen]] or [[given name]] of the [[Roman]] poet Marcus Valerius Martialis, born in [[Spain]] in the first century AD and noted for his [[epigrams]].

